# Proposal: a grid cell

You are shown the documented rules of the checks, not their code, and ONE cell of a grid under 'The cell to cover'. The cell says where a listed refusal phrase stands in a reply. The notation: P is one phrase from the list shown at the top of the rules, written exactly as the list has it; A is a small JSON object such as {"a": 1}; fence[ ... ] is a code fence, a line of three backticks before and after, holding what is named, one part per line; value(P) means P is the whole of a string value inside the object, as {"note": "P"}; key(P) means P is a key of the object, as {"P": 1}; 'then' means the next part goes on its own line below. Build the input exactly from the cell, nothing added. The rewritten text is the same reply with every occurrence of P replaced by words that are NOT on the list (for example 'I am not able to' in place of 'I cannot'), everything else identical character for character. Say what the rule, read as written, says the check's answer ought to do: move ('moves') if the phrase in that place counts, stay ('unchanged') if it does not. Your expectation comes from the rule's words alone: name in the body the words you read it from. Your kernel is conformance.kernel.refusal-phrase or conformance.kernel.response-verdict. The commitments are a STRING whose content is JSON of the form {"kernel": "<id>", "input": "<the input text>", "rewritten": "<your rewritten text>", "expect": "moves" or "unchanged", "rewrite": "<one sentence naming what you changed>", "cell": "<the cell exactly as named>"}. The input and the rewritten text are instances written out in full, never descriptions. Do not repeat a pair shown among the earlier proposals.

## The problem (problem)
The checks under test are the conformance harness's own functions. Most seats are shown only their documented rules, not their code: how the harness recovers a JSON object from a reply, whether it had to recover it from prose, its response verdict, its refusal-phrase check, and its grounding matcher. The question: is there an input and a rewrite of it on which the rule, read as written, says one thing and the check, run by machine, does another? The executor runs the check on both texts and records what its answer did; nothing here decides anything, the record shows where a reading and the machine parted.

